## Why this case is DUT-local, and why that is the right scope

  The other three cases in group C used a two-router topology with swi_a.  This one does not:
  - **swi_b has no VRF-Lite licence** (Base only; `VRF-LITE-FULL/63/8` is absent), so it cannot
    be a VRF peer at all;
  - swi_a does hold it, but it is under **RISK-1** (never reloaded or power-cycled), and a
    second-router limb would add nothing this case needs: VRF-Lite isolation is a property of
    **one router's forwarding tables**, and the sharpest test of it is two segments on ONE
    router that can and cannot reach each other depending only on VRF membership.
  ⇒ Before this case ran, group C's two-router topology was fully dismantled and **swi_a was
  restored to its pre-group baseline by configuration alone** — `tools/rcdiff.py` against
  `T43863/work/pre-u5.log` prints **nothing**, with no reload, so RISK-1 was honoured
  end to end.  The DUT was returned to the shared B/C/D underlay and its STP baseline
  (`Root Id 8000:0000cd423029` = swi_b, `port1.0.2` Rootport Forwarding, `port1.0.4/14/16`
  Alternate Discarding, **`port1.0.10` and `port1.0.48` Designated Forwarding**), exactly as
  triage recorded it.  This case therefore also IS group C's topology restore, verified.

