    awplus#show ip interface brief
    [VRF: red]    vlan10   10.38.22.34/27  admin up  running
    [VRF: blue]   vlan48   10.38.22.66/27  admin up  running

  (x960.md 7B.7 confirmed again: `ip vrf <name>` with no LO number auto-creates `lo1`/`lo2`,
  and `ip vrf forwarding` removes the interface's addresses so they must be re-applied.)

    swi_d(config)#ip multicast-routing vrf red
    swi_d(config)#ip multicast-routing vrf blue
    swi_d(config-if)#interface vlan10 / ip pim sparse-mode / ip igmp     <- `ip igmp` per F-13
    swi_d(config-if)#interface vlan48 / ip pim sparse-mode / ip igmp
    swi_d(config)#ip pim vrf red  rp-address 10.38.22.34
    swi_d(config)#ip pim vrf blue rp-address 10.38.22.66
    awplus#show running-config | include multicast
    no ip multicast-routing              <- the GLOBAL table stays off ...
    ip multicast-routing vrf red         <- ... while two VRFs have it on
    ip multicast-routing vrf blue

  🟢 That three-line read-back is itself a result: **multicast routing is genuinely per-VRF and
  independent of the global table.**  `ip multicast-routing vrf <name>` is not a modifier on a
  global switch; the global switch remained `no ip multicast-routing` for the whole case.

## Measurement stance

  As the rest of group C: `tools/arpleg.py` legs from host eth3 counted on host eth4, with a
  never-joined control group and an **instrument control**.
  🔴 **The instrument control needed rethinking for this case, and the first attempt was wrong.**
  Under VRF isolation *nothing* from eth3 may reach eth4 — including the ICMP control — so the
  usual "a flow that must arrive on the far NIC" is unavailable by construction.  An ICMP to the
  DUT's own red SVI was tried and produced **no reply**: the DUT would have to ARP for the
  crafted sender 10.38.22.40 and nothing answers that ARP (x960.md §7.6 / `arpleg.py --answer`).
  The liveness proof actually used is better suited to this case: **the DUT's own PIM hellos**.
  With PIM enabled per VRF, the red SVI sends hellos on eth3's wire and the **blue SVI sends
  them on eth4's wire**, so an isolation leg that captures `IP p103 10.38.22.66 -> 224.0.0.13`
  on eth4 while counting zero subject frames has proved its own capture alive from traffic the
  DUT generated.  Every isolation leg below carries that proof.  The second control is the
  **A/B**: the identical leg run again with the only change being vlan48's VRF membership.

