## LEG 2 (designed) — ISOLATION: source in VRF red, receiver in VRF blue

  Receiver joined 239.30.30.30 on eth4, which is in **blue**; the source sends from eth3, which
  is in **red**.  Per-VRF IGMP confirms the membership is held in blue:

    awplus#show ip igmp vrf blue groups
    Group Address    Interface            Uptime   Expires  Last Reporter
    239.30.30.30     vlan48               00:00:06 00:04:15 10.38.22.70
    awplus#show ip mroute vrf blue -> IP Multicast Routing Table [VRF: blue]      (empty)
    awplus#show ip mroute vrf red  -> IP Multicast Routing Table [VRF: red]       (empty)

    cap v2-vrf-isolation-eth3.pcap: 21 frame(s)
      x10 UDP  10.38.22.40 -> 239.30.30.30 :5001 [ours] [eth ... -> 01:00:5e:1e:1e:1e]
      x10 ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours]
      x1  IP p103 10.38.22.34 -> 224.0.0.13   <- the RED SVI's own PIM hello
    cap v2-vrf-isolation-eth4.pcap: 1 frame(s)
      x1  IP p103 10.38.22.66 -> 224.0.0.13   <- the BLUE SVI's own PIM hello: the capture IS live

  => **0 of 10 multicast and 0 of 10 unicast** crossed the VRF boundary, on a capture proved
     live by the DUT's own hello.  Both VRFs are running PIM independently on their own
     interfaces at the same instant.
  Repeated later with a 40 s window, after the F-9 check below, to catch more hellos:
    cap v6-f9-confirm-eth4.pcap: 2 frame(s)
      x2  IP p103 10.38.22.66 -> 224.0.0.13     and 0 of 10 group, 0 of 10 ICMP
  VERDICT leg 2: PASS — multicast is isolated by VRF, and so is the unicast beside it.

