## LEG 4 (designed) — per-VRF tables, and one read-back worth knowing about

    awplus#show ip mroute vrf red
    IP Multicast Routing Table
    [VRF: red]
    (10.38.22.40, 239.30.30.30), uptime 00:02:12   Owner PIM-SM, Flags: TF
      Incoming interface: vlan10      Outgoing interface list: vlan48 (1)
    (10.38.22.40, 239.31.31.31), uptime 00:00:34   Owner PIM-SM, Flags: TF
      Incoming interface: vlan10      Outgoing interface list:          <- the control: EMPTY

  ⚠️ **O-29**: `show ip mroute` **with no `vrf` keyword prints the VRF sections too**, headed
  `[VRF: red]`, and its output here was identical to `show ip mroute vrf red`.  The global table
  was empty (global multicast routing is off), so a reader skimming `show ip mroute` would
  reasonably but wrongly conclude these entries are in the global table.  **Read the `[VRF: ...]`
  heading.**  This differs from the unicast `show ip route`, where §7B.7 records the global
  table proper plus `[VRF: name]` sections.
  VERDICT leg 4: PASS.

## LEG 5 (designed, and the queue asked for it specifically) — the F-9 check

  **F-9**, carried from the previous campaign: removing an inter-VRF *unicast* static route left
  the dataplane forwarding across the VRF boundary from a stale ARP-derived per-host entry until
  `clear arp-cache vrf` was run — config and route table both said closed while traffic flowed.
  The queue asked whether the multicast path does the same.  Tested in the sharpest form: with
  (10.38.22.40, 239.30.30.30) actively forwarding and its OIF list holding `vlan48 (1)`,
  vlan48 was moved straight back into blue and the leg re-run **immediately, with no re-join and
  no `clear` of anything**:

    awplus(config-if)#no ip vrf forwarding red / ip vrf forwarding blue / ip address 10.38.22.66/27
    cap v5-f9-check-eth4.pcap: 0 frame(s)
    awplus#show ip mroute vrf red
    (10.38.22.40, 239.30.30.30), uptime 00:03:18   Owner PIM-SM, Flags: TF
      Incoming interface: vlan10      Outgoing interface list:          <- EMPTIED immediately
    (10.38.22.40, 239.31.31.31) ...   Outgoing interface list:

  => 🟢 **F-9's shape does NOT occur in the multicast path.** The outgoing-interface list emptied
     the moment the interface left the VRF and the dataplane stopped in the same instant — no
     stale forwarding, nothing to clear.  Confirmed by the 40 s re-run (leg 2 above) whose eth4
     capture held two of the DUT's own PIM hellos and zero subject frames.
  This is a genuinely useful negative: the multicast OIF is torn down with the interface, whereas
  the unicast hazard of F-9 lives in an ARP-derived host entry that outlives its route.
  VERDICT leg 5: PASS (and F-9 answered for this path).

